\section{Choose the Smallest Useful Native Surface}

Build one interpreter extension for a rolling numerical
summary. The native library accumulates an unsigned sample
count and a real-valued sum. The public interpreter methods
are \texttt{open\_meter()}, \texttt{feed(m, bytes)},
\texttt{mean(m)}, and \texttt{close(m)}. Each sample is a
little-endian unsigned 16-bit integer. The public buffer is
binary, not a UTF-8 string; a zero byte is a perfectly
ordinary byte. The native library takes an opaque meter
pointer, a byte pointer and a byte count. It rejects an odd
byte count and count overflow without changing the meter.
It never retains the byte pointer and all calls are on the
VM owner thread. The outer wrapper does not need a general
foreign declaration language to implement this interface.

Use compiled C stubs for the raw calls. Including the real
meter header lets the target compiler pass a \texttt{size\_t}
in its native representation while the interpreter-facing
entry checks a host buffer. The native query writes the mean
through a \texttt{double *} on success and returns
\texttt{EMPTY} without initializing it otherwise. Avoid
passing library records by value and avoid making either
side depend on the layout of the other side's objects.
Expose the meter as VM userdata or a dedicated foreign object,
not as an integer address a script can change.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lp{0.62\linewidth}}
\hline
Public operation & Successful effect or defined alternate result \\
\hline
\texttt{open\_meter} & Own a new meter initialized to count zero. \\
\texttt{feed} & Append the complete sequence or leave state unchanged. \\
\texttt{mean} & Return a real, or an empty option for count zero. \\
\texttt{close} & Remove the pointer and destroy once; repeat is harmless. \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\section{Define a Stack Shape at Each Exit}

Use an interpreter with a C API resembling the Lua virtual
stack in the local embedding extract. The extract shows
arguments and results moved through stack operations, and
userdata used to associate native storage with host values.
The survey of C APIs in extension languages contrasts this
arrangement with exposing handles in C variables. Here the
call frame has the meter and byte objects in argument slots
one and two. On success \texttt{feed} pushes the number of
samples accepted. On recoverable failure it pushes a
distinguished absence plus an error object. This is an
invented wrapper convention: the count returned to the
interpreter is the number of result slots, not a native
function's status or the number of samples. A stack
discipline error can return a wrong value while the native
state is otherwise perfectly consistent.

\Cref{fig:visual-396} shows the path from argument slots to
native state. The callback and thread cases from
\Cref{ch:ext-390,ch:ext-391} are excluded here by contract.
The meter object owns the native meter; the byte object
does not. Leases follow the bounded copy-or-pin model of
\Cref{ch:ext-389}, reflecting the distinction between
copies and reference objects in Crestani's survey.

