% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Implement Feed and Keep the Commit Point Visible}

\begin{verbatim}
vm_feed(vm):
    require_owner_thread(vm)
    meter := check_open_userdata(vm, 1, MeterKind)
    bytes := check_byte_object(vm, 2)
    size := checked_c_size_t(byte_length(bytes))
    if size % 2 != 0: return vm_push_error(vm, OddLength)
    samples := size / 2
    # Reserve result resources before native mutation.
    success := prepare_pushable_integer(vm, samples)
    with stable_read_only_bytes(bytes) as view:
        status := raw_meter_feed(meter.ptr, view.data, size)
    if status != OK: return vm_push_error(vm, translate(status))
    push_prepared_no_fail(vm, success)
    return 1

raw_meter_feed(m, data, size):
    if size % 2 != 0: return ODD_LENGTH
    n := size / 2
    if n > MAX_COUNT - m.count: return LIMIT
    extra := 0.0
    for i in 0 .. n - 1:
        extra += data[2*i] + 256 * data[2*i + 1]
    m.sum += extra
    m.count += n
    return OK
\end{verbatim}

The native loop reads two bytes independently; casting the
buffer to a native 16-bit integer pointer would introduce
endianness and alignment assumptions. The zero-length
case runs no iterations; the native API explicitly
permits null for that case. The example uses floating
accumulation and does not promise exact large sums.
Preparation of the interpreter result must also reserve
enough VM stack space and root any boxed value across the
call. If the implementation leaves result boxing until
after commit, a host allocation failure can cause a caller
to retry a batch the library already counted. Either
avoid that failure window or document a weaker contract.

\section{Finish the Other Entry Points and Test the Composition}

For open, construct and initialize empty userdata with
its finalizer before acquiring a native meter. Store the
returned pointer before doing anything that can fail. An
explicit close takes that pointer out and destroys it;
the finalizer follows the same path and sees an empty
object after explicit close. There is no reentrant native
execution in this design. If the library later starts
calling user handlers, this simple owner-thread argument
does not establish lease or teardown safety. For mean,
read the out cell only on success, and map \texttt{EMPTY}
to the empty option without ever inspecting the cell.

Start the end-to-end test by querying a fresh meter. Then
feed the byte pairs \((0,1)\) and \((2,0)\), which encode
256 and 2 and therefore yield mean 129. An unaligned
view of those four bytes must yield the same result. Pass
an odd-length slice next and verify that the mean is still
129; next use a native fixture seeded near the count limit
and ensure the overflow status does not change the meter.
Test allocation failure before mutation and inspect the
interpreter stack shape on every path. Finally close
twice and trigger finalization, verifying that the native
destroy routine ran exactly once and that further queries
reject the closed object without reaching C.

\section{Extend the Trace to Cover Identity and Ownership}

Open a second meter before closing the first one. Feed it
the pair \((4,0)\) and query it between the first meter's
feed and close. Its mean must be 4; the earlier mean of 129
belongs only to the first owner. Force the native allocator
to reuse the first meter's address on a subsequent open.
Keeping the old userdata in a script variable must not allow
it to read the new meter. Ownership is attached to the
userdata instance, and close already removed its pointer;
pointer equality is not an object-identity test.

Use an instrumented VM adapter to track the result slots
as well as the native allocator. Before feed, the adapter
records two argument slots and a rooted prepared result.
On success it observes one result slot with integer 2.
On odd input it observes absence and a descriptive error,
not a return count of zero masquerading as a stack count.
While injecting preparation failure, verify both that no
native feed ran and that the prepared slot was not exposed.
The stack trace and native state trace establish different
halves of the interface; neither replaces the other.

Treat the binding as a module with a native lifetime:
do not unregister or unload the meter implementation while
any userdata may invoke its finalizer. If the interpreter
supports unloading, keep the library alive through all
open meters, or reject unloading until the host has closed
and invalidated those objects. In a statically linked
interpreter this obligation is trivial, but the identical
public interface in a dynamically loaded interpreter may
require an explicit module lease as in \Cref{ch:ext-387}.
